& [SJS]
  + Situation: compact handles name exact checkpoints but no current component resolves those names to retained values
  + Justification: an immutable ordered map demonstrates the hash-table lookup contract without opening database persistence or concurrency scope
  + Solution: register exact handle-checkpoint pairs under digest keys and prove lookup plus canonical resume across the full fixture corpus

& [Delineation]
  + registry internal coherence proves its retained values agree and not that they came from the canonical fixture
  + scripted registry validation separately proves equality with all expected corpus registrations
  + lookup returns exact bytes and does not regenerate checkpoint semantics from a digest
  + in-memory value ownership is not filesystem database or service persistence

& [DataDrivenDesign]
  + BTreeMap fixes deterministic key order
  + key is exact lowercase SHA-256 checkpoint digest value
  + entry digest commits handle and checkpoint while registry root commits the complete ordered map
  + immutable registration returns a successor registry and leaves predecessor unchanged

& [FacultyReview]
  + Honesty separates internal custody coherence canonical corpus binding and physical persistence
  + Security validates predecessor map handle-checkpoint binding vacancy and successor root
  + Planner fixes registration order and typed iteration versus terminal resume
  + Weaver resolves minimal handles to exact retained checkpoints
  + Scribe preserves entry and root commitments
  + Refiner makes hash lookup operational without repeating checkpoints in the caller
  + Observer compares all twelve resolved resumes with uninterrupted fixture traces

& [Decision]
